{
  // The viewer's two scripts, type-checked.
  //
  // `app.js` and `sw.js` are hand-written browser JavaScript and will stay
  // that way: they are served as they are, by a Go-less server that reads
  // this directory off disk and by a build that copies it into the PWA, so
  // there is no step between this file and the browser and nothing to
  // compile. That is why they were outside the TypeScript project — and it
  // is also how a `$('#about')` that answers null in one of the two shells
  // reached a phone. `checkJs` puts them under the same compiler the rest
  // of the tree is held to, without changing what is shipped.
  //
  // What is on:
  //
  //   - `strictNullChecks`, which is the point of the exercise. Every
  //     `querySelector` is `… | null` and every use of one has to say what
  //     it does about that, so an element this file reaches for and a shell
  //     that does not have it is a compile error rather than a TypeError on
  //     somebody's phone.
  //   - `noImplicitThis` and the rest of `strict` minus `noImplicitAny`.
  //     Annotating every event handler's parameter would be several hundred
  //     JSDoc comments in a file whose comments are prose, for a class of
  //     bug the DOM's own types already catch at the call sites that matter.
  //
  // `types: []` keeps `@types/node` out: nothing here runs in Node, and a
  // `setTimeout` that returns a `Timeout` rather than a number is exactly
  // the wrong answer for a file that runs in a browser.
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ES2023",
    "lib": ["ES2023", "DOM", "DOM.Iterable"],
    "module": "preserve",
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",
    "types": [],
    "allowJs": true,
    "checkJs": true,
    "noEmit": true,
    "strictNullChecks": true,
    "strictFunctionTypes": true,
    "strictBindCallApply": true,
    "noImplicitThis": true,
    "useUnknownInCatchVariables": true,
    "alwaysStrict": true
  },
  "include": ["app.js"]
}
